# MaintMode — self-hosted environment.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Then fill in every value marked REQUIRED below. `docker compose up` fails
# fast and names the variable if one is missing, so you cannot half-configure
# your way into a broken instance.
#
# .env is gitignored. Keep it that way — it holds your OAuth client secret and
# your database password.
#
# NOTE: this file configures the frontend and the database. The BACKEND reads
# its own secrets from config/app.secrets.yaml, which you create separately —
# see README.md, "Generate secrets". The database password appears in BOTH
# places and the two must match.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# REQUIRED. Password for the `maintmode` Postgres role.
#
#   openssl rand -hex 32
#
# This value is baked into the database on FIRST start only. Changing it later
# does not change the password Postgres actually enforces — you would have to
# ALTER the role by hand (or delete the volume and lose your data). Pick it
# once, and copy the same value into config/app.secrets.yaml under `db/dsn`.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Frontend auth shell
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# REQUIRED. Signs the browser session cookie. Minimum 32 characters; the
# frontend refuses to start below that.
#
#   openssl rand -hex 32
#
# Rotating this invalidates every logged-in session — which is exactly what you
# want if it ever leaks.
MAINTMODE_AUTH_SECRET=

# REQUIRED. The public URL users type into a browser, with no trailing slash.
#
# This is NOT cosmetic. It is the origin the OAuth redirect is built from, so
# it must match both what the browser sees and the redirect URI you registered
# in Google Cloud Console. If they disagree, login fails with
# redirect_uri_mismatch.
#
#   Local, no proxy:       http://localhost:3000
#   Behind TLS on a host:  https://maintmode.example.com
MAINTMODE_APP_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Google OAuth
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# REQUIRED, both. Google is currently the only way to sign in, so an instance
# without these has no login at all.
#
# Get them from Google Cloud Console. The full walkthrough is in README.md
# under "Set up Google OAuth" — read it, this is where most installs stall.
#
# The one thing to get right: the Authorized redirect URI registered in Google
# must be exactly
#
#   <MAINTMODE_APP_BASE_URL>/api/auth/callback/google
#
# The client ID is not a secret (it travels in every browser redirect). The
# client secret is — it stays on the server and is never sent to a browser.
MAINTMODE_GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
MAINTMODE_GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Networking (optional)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Host interface the frontend port binds to. Defaults to 127.0.0.1 — reachable
# from the host itself and from a reverse proxy running on it, but NOT from the
# network. This is deliberate: on a fresh install the first person to log in
# through Google becomes the administrator, so an instance must not be publicly
# reachable before you have logged in yourself.
#
# Set to 0.0.0.0 to expose it directly, but only after you have bootstrapped —
# and preferably never, in favour of a reverse proxy terminating TLS.
# MAINTMODE_BIND_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1

# Host port for the frontend. Change it if 3000 is already taken. Note this
# changes only the HOST side, so if you are not behind a proxy you must update
# MAINTMODE_APP_BASE_URL and the Google redirect URI to match.
# MAINTMODE_HTTP_PORT=3000

# How long the frontend waits on a backend call, in milliseconds.
# MAINTMODE_API_TIMEOUT_MS=10000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Image versions
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CI publishes images tagged by branch (`main`), by release tag (`v1.2.3`) and
# by commit (`sha-abc1234`). There is no `latest` tag, so pin something real.
# `main` tracks the newest build; a release tag is what you want in production,
# because it is the only one that does not move under you.
MAINTMODE_VERSION=main
MAINTMODE_UI_VERSION=main
